+
+# Critical
+
+## C-1 — `upgradeTo` has no access control
+
+**Contract · function:** `LendingMarket.upgradeTo` (`src/LendingMarket.sol:84`)
+
+```solidity
+function upgradeTo(address newImplementation) external {
+    assembly { sstore(IMPLEMENTATION_SLOT, newImplementation) }
+    emit Upgraded(newImplementation);
+}
+```
+
+There is no `onlyOwner`. `MarketProxy` delegates all calls to the implementation, and the
+implementation owns the upgrade entry point (`src/MarketProxy.sol:4` says so explicitly),
+so this function is reachable through the proxy by **any address on Arbitrum**.
+
+**What the attacker gets:** everything. They deploy an implementation whose fallback
+transfers out the market's USDC and calls `vault.seize(...)` for every depositor, point the
+proxy at it, and empty both the market's USDC balance and the entire collateral vault in one
+transaction. `CollateralVault.seize` is gated on `onlyMarket` — but the attacker *is* the
+market at that point. This is a complete, unconditional loss of all user funds and requires
+no capital.
+
+**Fix:** add `onlyOwner`. Better, drop the hand-rolled slot write and inherit OpenZeppelin's
+`UUPSUpgradeable`, implementing `_authorizeUpgrade` as `onlyOwner`. Put the owner key behind
+a multisig, and behind a timelock before mainnet-scale TVL.
+
+---
+
+## C-2 — `setOracle` has no access control
+
+**Contract · function:** `LendingMarket.setOracle` (`src/LendingMarket.sol:91`)
+
+```solidity
+function setOracle(PriceOracle newOracle) external {
+    oracle = newOracle;
+```
+
+Same omission as C-1. Note the contrast with `setBorrowRate` and `listCollateral` two lines
+below, which *are* `onlyOwner` — the modifier was simply dropped here.
+
+**What the attacker gets:** they deploy a contract with a `getPrice(address)` that returns
+`1e30` for wstETH and the honest price for USDC, call `setOracle`, deposit one wei of
+wstETH, and borrow the market's entire USDC balance — `healthFactor` will read as healthy.
+Alternatively they invert it: report wstETH at near-zero, then call `liquidateAll` and buy
+every depositor's collateral at a 5% discount to a fabricated price. Either direction is
+total loss of the market's USDC or of all deposited collateral.
+
+**Fix:** `onlyOwner`. Additionally, oracle replacement is a high-blast-radius action —
+route it through a timelock so depositors can exit if governance is compromised.
+
+---
+
+## C-3 — `setLiquidationThreshold` has no access control
+
+**Contract · function:** `LendingMarket.setLiquidationThreshold` (`src/LendingMarket.sol:96`)
+
+```solidity
+function setLiquidationThreshold(uint256 bps) external {
+    liquidationThreshold = bps;
+```
+
+Unprotected, and additionally unbounded (see M-8).
+
+**What the attacker gets:** two directions, both fatal.
+- Set it to e.g. `1_000_000` bps. `healthFactor` scales linearly with it, so every position
+  reads as massively healthy. Deposit dust, borrow the entire USDC balance, walk away.
+- Set it to `0`. Every position with any debt returns `healthFactor == 0` and becomes
+  liquidatable. Call `liquidateAll` and seize every borrower's collateral at a 5% bonus to
+  the honest price.
+
+**Fix:** `onlyOwner`, plus bound the input (`bps > 0 && bps <= BPS`, and realistically
+`<= 9_500`). Call `accrueInterest()` first so the change does not retroactively reprice
+already-accrued debt. Consider requiring that the new threshold not be lowered by more than
+some delta per period, so a compromised owner cannot mass-liquidate in one transaction.
+
