+
+## C-5 — `healthFactor` divides before multiplying, liquidating solvent users
+
+**Contract · function:** `LendingMarket.healthFactor` (`src/LendingMarket.sol:146`)
+
+```solidity
+return (collateralValueUsd(user) / debtUsd) * liquidationThreshold * WAD / BPS;
+```
+
+The integer division `collateralValueUsd / debtUsd` is performed **first**, truncating the
+collateral ratio to a whole number before any scaling is applied. The health factor can
+therefore only ever take the values `0, 0.8e18, 1.6e18, 2.4e18, …` — multiples of the
+threshold. There is no precision between them.
+
+**What the unlucky user gets:** liquidated while sitting at nearly twice the required
+collateral.
+
+| Collateral / debt | True HF (80% threshold) | HF as computed | Liquidatable? |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| 1.30× | 1.04 | `1 × 0.8` = 0.80 | **yes** (should be no) |
+| 1.90× | 1.52 | `1 × 0.8` = 0.80 | **yes** (should be no) |
+| 1.99× | 1.59 | `1 × 0.8` = 0.80 | **yes** (should be no) |
+| 2.00× | 1.60 | `2 × 0.8` = 1.60 | no |
+
+Two consequences. First, `_borrow` reverts below `1e18`, so the **effective maximum LTV is
+50%, not the advertised 80%** — the product does not do what the README says it does.
+Second, and much worse: a borrower who opens a position at exactly 2.0× collateral is one
+tick of price movement away from the cliff. A **0.5% adverse price move** takes them from
+`HF = 1.60` to `HF = 0.80`, and because there is no close factor (H-5) they lose their
+*entire* position at a 5% penalty. An attacker who can nudge the position across that
+boundary — or simply a keeper watching the feed — collects the bonus on a position that was
+never actually undercollateralised.
+
+**Fix:** multiply before dividing, single division at the end:
+
+```solidity
+return collateralValueUsd(user) * liquidationThreshold * WAD / (BPS * debtUsd);
+```
+
+Both `collateralValueUsd` and `debtValueUsd` are already 1e18-scaled, so this is
+dimensionally correct as written. There is no realistic overflow risk in 256 bits at these
+magnitudes. Once fixed, re-derive the liquidation cliff and confirm the 80% threshold
+actually binds where you expect it to — this bug has almost certainly been masking whatever
+manual testing was done against the threshold.
+
