+# High
+
+## H-1 — No Arbitrum sequencer-uptime check
+
+**Contract · function:** `PriceOracle.getPrice` (`src/PriceOracle.sol:39`)
+
+This is specific to the stated deployment target. On Arbitrum One, if the sequencer goes
+down, Chainlink feeds stop updating while the L2 keeps its last-known prices. When the
+sequencer resumes, there is a window in which users can transact against prices that predate
+the outage before the feeds catch up. Chainlink's own guidance for every L2 deployment is to
+gate price reads on the **L2 Sequencer Uptime Feed**
+(`0xFdB631F5EE196F0ed6FAa767959853A9F217697D` on Arbitrum One).
+
+**What the attacker gets:** they queue transactions to execute the instant the sequencer
+restarts. If the real price moved during the downtime, they borrow against collateral valued
+at the pre-outage price, or liquidate positions that are healthy at the true current price.
+Honest users get the mirror image: liquidated on a price that was never real, with no
+opportunity to add collateral because the chain was not accepting their transactions.
+
+**Fix:** read the uptime feed, revert if `answer == 1` (sequencer down), and additionally
+revert if `block.timestamp - startedAt < GRACE_PERIOD` (3600s is the common choice) so users
+have time to react after a restart. Ideally pair this with a pause (M-5) so the team can also
+halt liquidations manually.
+
+---
+
+## H-2 — wstETH collateral is priced off the stETH/USD feed
+
+**Contract · function:** `PriceOracle` configuration, consumed by
+`LendingMarket.collateralValueUsd` (`src/LendingMarket.sol:137`)
+
+Cross-checked the README table against Arbitrum One. The token address
+`0x5979D7b546E38E414F7E9822514be443A4800529` is wstETH and
+`0x07C5b924399cc23c24a95c8743DE4006a32b7f2a` is the STETH/USD aggregator. Both addresses are
+individually correct — but they are **not the same asset**.
+
+wstETH is the non-rebasing wrapper: one wstETH is worth `stETH/USD × wstETH-per-stETH`
+exchange rate, and that rate is strictly greater than 1 and monotonically increasing as
+staking rewards accrue (roughly 1.2 today, drifting up ~3–4% a year). Pricing wstETH at the
+raw stETH price therefore **undervalues every depositor's collateral by ~17%, and the error
+grows over time.**
+
+**What the unlucky user gets:** less borrowing power than they are entitled to, and — more
+seriously — liquidation roughly 17% earlier than the stated 80% threshold implies. A user who
+believes they are at 70% LTV is treated by the contract as being at ~84% and gets seized.
+Because the discrepancy is systematic rather than random, this affects every wstETH depositor
+simultaneously on any downward move. Stacked on C-5, which already quantises the health factor
+in 0.8 steps, the real liquidation boundary bears no resemblance to the documented one.
+
+Note the direction is conservative for protocol solvency — it does not create bad debt — which
+is exactly why it will not show up in a smoke test and will instead surface as user losses.
+
+**Fix:** use the **wstETH/USD** feed on Arbitrum One
+(`0xB1552C5e96B312d0Bf8b554186F846C40614a540`), or compose stETH/USD with wstETH's on-chain
+`stEthPerToken()` exchange rate. If you compose, guard the exchange-rate read the same way you
+guard a price feed — bound it, and never let it move more than a plausible amount per block.
+
+More generally: this is the first entry in a class. weETH and ezETH on the shortlist are also
+wrappers whose value is a rate on top of an underlying, and both have exactly the same failure
+mode. Add a listing checklist item that names the feed's `description()` and asserts it matches
+the collateral token before governance signs the `listCollateral` transaction.
+
+---
+
+## H-3 — The `borrowers` array grows without bound; `liquidateAll` can be bricked
+
+**Contract · function:** `LendingMarket._borrow` (`src/LendingMarket.sol:200`) and
+`LendingMarket.liquidateAll` (`src/LendingMarket.sol:190`)
+
+```solidity
+if (principalOf[borrower] == 0) borrowers.push(borrower);
+principalOf[borrower] += amount * WAD / borrowIndex;
+```
+
+Entries are pushed but never removed — not on `repay`, not on `_liquidate` (which sets
+`principalOf[user] = 0` on line 214 without pruning). So repay-then-reborrow duplicates a
+user, and every liquidated user leaves a permanent entry behind.
+
+Worse, the guard is on `principalOf == 0`, and **`borrow(0)` leaves it at zero**. So does any
+`amount` small enough that `amount * WAD / borrowIndex` floors to zero. Each such call
+appends another entry.
+
+**What the attacker gets:** a permanent denial of service on the protocol's only liquidation
+sweep, for the price of gas on Arbitrum — which is cheap enough to make this trivially
+affordable. They loop `borrow(0)` until `borrowers.length` is large enough that
+`liquidateAll` exceeds the block gas limit. `liquidateAll` iterates the whole array and calls
+`healthFactor` on each entry, which itself loops every collateral token and makes two external
+oracle calls per token — so the per-entry cost is high and the array does not need to be very
+large. Once over the limit it can never be called again, because there is no way to shrink the
+array. The keeper described in the README as the response to "a large price move" is
+permanently disabled, and the market accrues bad debt on the next volatility event.
+
+`liquidateAll` has a second, independent fragility: it reverts atomically if any single
+`_liquidate` reverts — e.g. the caller's USDC allowance runs out midway, or one collateral
+transfer fails. One bad position blocks the sweep for everyone.
+
+**Fix:** three parts.
+1. Reject zero and dust borrows: `if (amount == 0) revert ZeroAmount();` and require the
+   resulting principal delta to be non-zero.
+2. Track membership properly — `mapping(address => uint256) borrowerIndex` with swap-and-pop
+   removal when `principalOf` reaches zero in `repay` and `_liquidate` — so the array reflects
+   only live positions.
+3. Delete `liquidateAll` entirely. An unbounded loop over all borrowers is not a safe on-chain
+   primitive at any array size. Replace it with `liquidate(address[] calldata users)` where the
+   keeper supplies the batch off-chain and each element is `try`/`catch`-wrapped so one failure
+   does not revert the batch. This also removes the need for (2) to be gas-perfect.
+
