+
+## H-4 — ERC-20 return values are never checked
+
+**Contract · functions:** `LendingMarket._borrow:204`, `LendingMarket.repay:177`,
+`LendingMarket._liquidate:213`, `CollateralVault.deposit:38`, `CollateralVault.withdraw:48`,
+`CollateralVault.seize:57`
+
+Every transfer in the codebase discards the boolean:
+
+```solidity
+debtAsset.transfer(borrower, amount);
+IERC20(token).transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount);
+```
+
+Two distinct failure modes:
+
+- **Tokens that return `false` instead of reverting.** The call succeeds, the return value is
+  ignored, and state is updated as if the transfer happened. In `CollateralVault.deposit` that
+  credits a user collateral they never delivered — which they can then borrow against, and the
+  loss lands on the market. Native USDC on Arbitrum reverts rather than returning false, so
+  this is not live at launch, but it is one listing away.
+
+- **Tokens that return nothing at all.** `IERC20` in `src/interfaces/IERC20.sol:15` declares
+  `transferFrom` as returning `bool`, so Solidity emits a `returndatasize` check and **reverts**
+  when a token returns an empty buffer. **USDT is exactly this token, and it is named on the
+  shortlist in the README.** Listing USDT against this interface produces a collateral type
+  that can be deposited by nobody — every `deposit` reverts — and if it is ever listed while
+  positions exist, `_liquidate` will revert on the USDT leg and take `liquidateAll` down with it
+  (H-3). The failure is total and will look baffling in production.
+
+**Fix:** use OpenZeppelin's `SafeERC20` (`safeTransfer` / `safeTransferFrom`) at all six call
+sites. It handles both the false-return and the empty-return cases. This is a prerequisite for
+the USDT listing, not an optional hardening step.
+
+---
+
+## H-5 — Liquidation closes 100% of the debt and silently under-seizes
+
+**Contract · function:** `LendingMarket._liquidate` (`src/LendingMarket.sol:209`)
+
+```solidity
+uint256 debt = debtOf(user);
+debtAsset.transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), debt);
+principalOf[user] = 0;
+...
+for (uint256 i = 0; i < collateralTokens.length && seizeUsd > 0; i++) { ... }
+```
+
+Two problems in one function.
+
+**No close factor.** The liquidator repays the *entire* debt and seizes collateral worth
+`debt × 1.05`. Comparable markets cap a single liquidation at 50% of the debt precisely so a
+position that dips marginally below the threshold is nudged back to health rather than
+destroyed. Here, an unlucky user whose health factor touches `0.999e18` for one block loses
+their whole position and pays the full 5% penalty on all of it. Combined with C-5 — where the
+health factor jumps from 1.6 to 0.8 on a fraction of a percent of price movement — this means
+users are wiped out with no warning band at all. There is no partial-liquidation path and no
+way for the user to be liquidated *less*.
+
+**Silent under-seizure.** The loop terminates when it runs out of collateral tokens, leaving
+`seizeUsd > 0` with no revert and no accounting. The liquidator has already paid the full debt
+on line 213. If the position is underwater past the bonus — collateral worth less than
+`debt × 1.05`, which is precisely the situation in a fast crash — the liquidator pays 100% and
+receives less than 105%, so at some point they simply stop liquidating. The position sits with
+`principalOf` zeroed but the market's USDC never repaid. That shortfall is bad debt, it is
+recorded nowhere, and with no supply side in v1 it silently comes out of the treasury's
+principal.
+
+**Fix:**
+- Introduce a close factor: cap the repayable amount at `debt × closeFactor / BPS` (start at
+  5000) and take `repayAmount` as a caller parameter bounded by that, rather than always using
+  the full debt. Reduce `principalOf` proportionally instead of zeroing it.
+- When collateral is exhausted, either revert, or compute the actual USD seized and refund the
+  liquidator the unmatched portion — do not let the loop fall through silently.
+- Add explicit bad-debt accounting: a `totalBadDebt` accumulator plus an event when a position
+  is closed with a shortfall, so the treasury can see losses as they happen rather than
+  discovering them at withdrawal time.
+- Consider a liquidation-bonus ramp tied to how far underwater the position is, so deeply
+  underwater positions stay attractive to liquidate.
+
+---
+
+# Medium
+
+## M-1 — Interest is derived from `block.number × 12`
+
+**Contract · function:** `LendingMarket.accrueInterest` (`src/LendingMarket.sol:117`)
+
+```solidity
+uint256 secondsElapsed = blocksElapsed * SECONDS_PER_BLOCK;   // 12
+```
+
+On Arbitrum One, `block.number` returns an *approximation of the L1 block number*, updated
+lazily as the sequencer observes L1 — not the L2 block number, and not on a fixed cadence. The
+constant is therefore approximately right on average and wrong in any given interval: it drifts
+with L1 block-time variance, and it lags or jumps when the sequencer's view of L1 updates in
+chunks. Interest is systematically mis-accrued in whichever direction the drift runs, and
+borrowers or the treasury absorb the difference. There is no scenario where this constant is
+correct and `block.timestamp` is not.
+
+**Fix:** track `lastAccrualTimestamp` and use `block.timestamp - lastAccrualTimestamp`
+directly. Delete `SECONDS_PER_BLOCK`. Remember the append-only storage constraint — add a new
+timestamp variable rather than repurposing `lastAccrualBlock`.
+
+## M-2 — Withdrawal solvency check runs against a stale borrow index
+
+**Contract · function:** `CollateralVault.withdraw:50` → `LendingMarket.requireHealthy:153`
+
+`requireHealthy` is `view`, so it cannot call `accrueInterest()`. Every other entry point
+accrues first; this one does not. A user withdrawing collateral is checked against a debt
+figure that omits all interest since the last accrual, so they can withdraw more than they
+should and land just below the threshold once the index catches up.
+
+The window is small when the market is busy and unbounded when it is quiet — a low-activity
+period is exactly when nothing calls `accrueInterest`, and also exactly when this is easiest to
+exploit deliberately.
+
+**Fix:** have `CollateralVault.withdraw` call a non-view market function that accrues and then
+checks, e.g. `LendingMarket.accrueAndRequireHealthy(address)`. Alternatively expose a view
+`healthFactorWithAccrual()` that computes the pending index growth inline without writing it.
+
+## M-3 — Deposits credit the requested amount, not the amount received
+
+**Contract · function:** `CollateralVault.deposit` (`src/CollateralVault.sol:35`)
+
+```solidity
+IERC20(token).transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount);
+balanceOf[msg.sender][token] += amount;
+```
+
+For a fee-on-transfer token the vault credits more than it holds, and the last depositors to
+withdraw find the vault short. For a **rebasing** token — which the README says the DAO has
+explicitly asked for — the accounting is structurally wrong in both directions: positive
+rebases accrue to the vault contract and are unclaimable by anyone, and a negative rebase makes
+the sum of `balanceOf` exceed the real balance, so withdrawals fail for whoever is last.
+
+**Fix:** for the current model, measure the delta:
+`uint256 before = IERC20(token).balanceOf(address(this)); ... amount = after - before;` and
+credit that. For genuine rebasing support, move to share-based accounting (store shares, convert
+on read) — that is a design change, and it should be scoped before the DAO's request turns into
+a listing.
+
+## M-4 — `getPrice` reverts for any feed with more than 18 decimals
+
+**Contract · function:** `PriceOracle.getPrice` (`src/PriceOracle.sol:44`)
+
+`10 ** (18 - feed.decimals())` underflows and reverts when `decimals() > 18`. USD-denominated
+Chainlink feeds are 8 decimals so the launch config is fine — but **ETH-denominated feeds are
+18 decimals**, and the natural feeds for weETH and ezETH on the shortlist are ETH-denominated.
+Listing one bricks `getPrice` for that token, which bricks `collateralValueUsd`, which bricks
+`borrow`, `repay`, `liquidate` and `withdraw` for **every user in the market**, not just holders
+of that collateral — the loop on line 133 iterates all listed tokens.
+
+**Fix:** handle both directions explicitly (scale up or down depending on the sign of the
+difference), and validate `decimals()` in `setFeed` so an incompatible feed cannot be listed at
+all. If you adopt ETH-denominated feeds, you also need a composition step through ETH/USD.
+
+## M-5 — No delisting and no pause
+
+**Contract · function:** `LendingMarket.listCollateral` (`src/LendingMarket.sol:107`)
+
+`listCollateral` is one-way. `isCollateral` is never cleared, `collateralTokens` is never popped,
+and `vault.setSupported(token, false)` is never called by anything. There is also no pause on
+`borrow`, `repay`, `liquidate` or `deposit` anywhere in the system.
+
+If a listed collateral is compromised, its oracle feed is deprecated, or its price becomes
+unreliable, governance has **no response short of a full proxy upgrade** — and the upgrade path
+is itself currently the C-1 vulnerability. During an active incident that is the worst possible
+time to be writing and deploying a new implementation.
+
+**Fix:** add `unlistCollateral(address)` (owner-gated) that clears `isCollateral`, removes the
+entry from `collateralTokens`, and calls `vault.setSupported(token, false)` — note that
+withdrawals must keep working for delisted tokens so users can exit. Add a `Pausable` guard on
+the borrow and deposit paths. Deliberately leave `repay` and `withdraw` unpausable so a pause can
+never trap user funds.
+
+## M-6 — The treasury cannot withdraw principal or interest
+
+**Contract:** `LendingMarket`
+
+The README states the treasury seeds the market's USDC by direct transfer and "takes the
+interest." There is no function that moves USDC out of `LendingMarket` other than `_borrow`.
+Repayments and liquidation proceeds accumulate in the contract with no withdrawal path. The
+treasury's seed capital and all accrued interest are locked, recoverable only via an upgrade.
+
+**Fix:** add an owner-gated `withdrawReserves(uint256)`, bounded so it cannot draw down the
+liquidity backing outstanding debt — track `totalPrincipal` and refuse to withdraw below the
+amount needed to honour it. This is worth designing now rather than retrofitting, since the v2
+supply side will need the same accounting.
+
+## M-7 — The implementation contract is left uninitialized
+
+**Contract · function:** `LendingMarket.initialize` (`src/LendingMarket.sol:56`)
+
+`MarketProxy`'s constructor delegatecalls `initData`, so the proxy's storage is initialized
+atomically at deployment and cannot be front-run — that part is fine. But the implementation
+contract's *own* storage is never initialized, so anyone can call `initialize` directly on the
+implementation address and become its `owner`.
+
+Impact is limited today: the implementation holds no funds, and `selfdestruct` is no longer
+available in a way that would brick the proxy. It is still an unowned privileged entry point on
+a verified contract, it will confuse anyone reading the deployment, and it becomes genuinely
+dangerous if a future implementation ever adds a `delegatecall`.
+
+**Fix:** add a constructor to `LendingMarket` that locks initialization on the implementation
+(OZ's `_disableInitializers()`, or set `initialized = true` in the constructor — the latter is
+safe here because the proxy's storage is separate).
+
+## M-8 — Privileged setters are unbounded, and `liquidationBonus` has no setter
+
+**Contract · functions:** `LendingMarket.setBorrowRate:101`, `setLiquidationThreshold:96`
+
+Even once access control is fixed (C-3), neither setter validates its input. `setBorrowRate` can
+be set arbitrarily high; the next `accrueInterest` then inflates the borrow index enough to make
+every position instantly liquidatable. `setLiquidationThreshold` can be set to `0` or above
+`BPS`. A compromised or fat-fingered owner key wipes the market in one transaction.
+
+Separately, `liquidationBonus` is initialized to 500 in `initialize` and has **no setter at all**
+— it cannot be tuned without an upgrade, even though it is the parameter most likely to need
+adjustment if liquidations stop clearing.
+
+**Fix:** bound both setters (`borrowRate <= MAX_BORROW_RATE`, `0 < threshold <= 9_500`), add a
+bounded `setLiquidationBonus`, and emit the old and new values in the events for monitoring. Put
+parameter changes behind the same timelock as C-1/C-2.
+
+## M-9 — Ownership cannot be rotated on the market, and is footgunned on the oracle
+
+**Contract · functions:** `LendingMarket` (no transfer function),
+`PriceOracle.transferOwnership` (`src/PriceOracle.sol:28`)
+
+`LendingMarket.owner` is set once in `initialize` and there is no `transferOwnership`. If the
+owner key is lost or compromised, `setBorrowRate` and `listCollateral` are gone forever and the
+only recourse is an upgrade.
+
+`PriceOracle.transferOwnership` is one-step and does not reject `address(0)` — a single mistyped
+argument permanently disables `setFeed`, and with it any ability to respond to a feed
+deprecation.
+
+Note also that the oracle owner and the market owner are independent addresses with no
+enforced relationship, which is easy to get wrong at deployment.
+
+**Fix:** add two-step ownership transfer (`Ownable2Step`) to both contracts, with a zero-address
+check. Document which key controls which contract and confirm both are the intended multisig
+before deploying.
+
+## M-10 — `upgradeTo` does not verify the new implementation is a contract
+
+**Contract · function:** `LendingMarket.upgradeTo` (`src/LendingMarket.sol:84`)
+
+The raw `sstore` accepts any address. `delegatecall` to an address with no code returns
+*success* with empty returndata, so pointing the proxy at an EOA or a typo'd address does not
+revert — it makes every call to the proxy silently succeed and return nothing. Every position,
+every collateral balance and all USDC become permanently unreachable, and the failure is not
+detectable from the upgrade transaction's receipt.
+
+**Fix:** `if (newImplementation.code.length == 0) revert NotAContract();` before the store.
+Inheriting `UUPSUpgradeable` (C-1) gives you this plus the ERC-1967 rollback check for free.
+
